# Rule: credential_in_output
# Category: Credential Theft
# Severity: critical
# Attack Class: credential_theft
#
# Detects API keys, tokens, and other credentials appearing in tool response
# outputs. An agent that returns credentials in its output may have been
# tricked into reading secrets via prompt injection, or the underlying tool
# may be exposing sensitive data.
#
# Patterns detected:
#   - AWS access keys (AKIA...)
#   - AWS secret keys (40-char base64 after aws_secret)
#   - GitHub personal access tokens (ghp_, gho_, ghs_, ghr_)
#   - Generic API key patterns (api_key=..., bearer token)
#   - OpenAI keys (sk-...)
#   - Private key markers (BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY, BEGIN PRIVATE KEY)
#
# Note: This rule fires on TOOL_CALL events since tool_input is where
# extracted/returned data would appear. In Phase 2, TOOL_RESPONSE content
# monitoring will be added.

event.type == "TOOL_CALL"
AND regex_match("(?i)(AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}|ghp_[a-zA-Z0-9]{36}|gho_[a-zA-Z0-9]{36}|ghs_[a-zA-Z0-9]{36}|ghr_[a-zA-Z0-9]{36}|sk-[a-zA-Z0-9]{20,}|api[_-]?key\\s*[=:]\\s*['\"]?[a-zA-Z0-9]{16,}|bearer\\s+[a-zA-Z0-9._\\-]{20,}|-----BEGIN (RSA )?PRIVATE KEY-----|aws_secret_access_key\\s*[=:])", event.raw_data.tool_input)
